{
  "title": "Olecranon Fractures - Pediatric",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pediatrics/4010/olecranon-fractures--pediatric",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Dec 11 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Olecranon Fractures are rare fractures in the pediatric population and most commonly occur as a result of fall onto an outstretched hand with the elbow in flexion.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made with plain radiographs.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment may be nonoperative for nondisplaced fractures with an intact extensor mechanism. Surgical management is indicated for displaced fractures or fractures associated with loss of extensor mechanism.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**uncommon fracture** in children",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "in the US, accounts for <5% of all pediatric fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "peak age between 5-10 year old",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**fall onto outstretched arm** with",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**elbow in flexion (most common)**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "triceps and brachialis tensioning causes a transverse olecranon fracture",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [
                            {
                              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4010/images/olecranon_transverse_fx.jpg",
                              "caption": "lateral elbow showing a transverse olecranon fracture in a skeletally immature patient"
                            }
                          ],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**elbow in extension**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4010/images/longitudinal_fx.jpg",
                          "caption": "Longitudinal fracture line consistent with an extension type fracture to the olecranon"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "varus/valgus bending forces through the olecranon causes longitudinal fracture lines",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": [
                            {
                              "text": "varus may lead to associated radial head dislocation",
                              "table": null,
                              "images": [],
                              "importance": "1",
                              "children": []
                            },
                            {
                              "text": "valgus may lead to an associated fracture of the radial neck",
                              "table": null,
                              "images": [],
                              "importance": "1",
                              "children": []
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "direct trauma (least common)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "shear force creates anterior tension failure with anterior displacement of the distal fracture and intact posterior periosteum",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4010/images/anterior_olecranon_fracture.jpg",
                          "caption": "illustration showing shear injury to the olecranon with anterior displacement of the distal fracture"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "location",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**metaphyseal (most common)**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "physeal",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "epiphyseal (apophyseal)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "intra-articular",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "extra-articular",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**osteogenesis imperfecta**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "olecranon avulsion fractures are highly suspicious for osteogenesis imperfecta",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Ossification centers of elbow",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "age of ossification/appearance and **age of fusion** are two independent events that must be differentiated",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4010/images/cropped%20critol.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4010/images/cropped%20critol-2.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4010/images/ossifcation%20of%20the%20elbow_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4010/images/elbow%20ossification%20and%20fusion_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "olecranon apophysis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "ossifies/appears at age 9 years",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "fuses at age ~ 15 -17 years",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Olecranon ossification",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "fusion of the epiphysis to the metaphysis of the olecranon occurs from anterior to posterior",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "average age of closure is between the ages of 15-17 years old",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**partial closure may be mistaken for olecranon fracture**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "",
          "table": [
            {
